{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-64218" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $2\\,\\text{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075550/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce3cfd52bc88b8e65e4e21d07121bdfe56fe072f3559a7730c852f65ca34f434"} {"question" "q-64218" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude, and $ϕ$ is the phase. In our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=4\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0)=0.$ Substituing this into the equation gives\n\n$$\nR\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=0\\,⟹\\,ϕ=0.\n$$\n\nSo our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $2\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that ${y}^{′}(0)=2.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2R\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n2=2R\\,⟹\\,R=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $1\\,\\text{m}.$ The solution for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075550/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce3cfd52bc88b8e65e4e21d07121bdfe56fe072f3559a7730c852f65ca34f434"} {"question" "q-64218" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075550/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7cab2d136dfd5bf05edfe2405cc2501828de49319fb313615e7c1e87d4634ec1"} {"question" "q-64218" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075550/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce3cfd52bc88b8e65e4e21d07121bdfe56fe072f3559a7730c852f65ca34f434"} {"question" "q-64218" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "1\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "2\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "4\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075550/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce3cfd52bc88b8e65e4e21d07121bdfe56fe072f3559a7730c852f65ca34f434"} {"question" "q-64218" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1\\,\\text{m}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075550/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce3cfd52bc88b8e65e4e21d07121bdfe56fe072f3559a7730c852f65ca34f434"} {"question" "q-79494" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+81y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $3\\,\\text{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075550/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce3cfd52bc88b8e65e4e21d07121bdfe56fe072f3559a7730c852f65ca34f434"} {"question" "q-79494" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude, and $ϕ$ is the phase. In our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=81\\,⟹\\,\\omega =9.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0)=0.$ Substituing this into the equation gives\n\n$$\nR\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=0\\,⟹\\,ϕ=0.\n$$\n\nSo our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡9t.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $3\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that ${y}^{′}(0)=3.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=9R\\operatorname{cos}⁡9t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=3$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n3=9R\\,⟹\\,R=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{m}.$ The solution for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡9t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075550/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce3cfd52bc88b8e65e4e21d07121bdfe56fe072f3559a7730c852f65ca34f434"} {"question" "q-79494" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075550/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7cab2d136dfd5bf05edfe2405cc2501828de49319fb313615e7c1e87d4634ec1"} {"question" "q-79494" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075550/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce3cfd52bc88b8e65e4e21d07121bdfe56fe072f3559a7730c852f65ca34f434"} {"question" "q-79494" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{9}\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "\\frac{9}{2}\\,\\text{m}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075550/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce3cfd52bc88b8e65e4e21d07121bdfe56fe072f3559a7730c852f65ca34f434"} {"question" "q-79494" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{m}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075550/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce3cfd52bc88b8e65e4e21d07121bdfe56fe072f3559a7730c852f65ca34f434"} {"question" "q-94275" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$\n\nNote that $R,{ϕ}_{1},{ϕ}_{2},A$ and $B$ are constants." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075550/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce3cfd52bc88b8e65e4e21d07121bdfe56fe072f3559a7730c852f65ca34f434"} {"question" "q-94275" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=4\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$\n\nTherefore, only function III could be used to represent $y(t).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075550/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce3cfd52bc88b8e65e4e21d07121bdfe56fe072f3559a7730c852f65ca34f434"} {"question" "q-94275" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075550/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7cab2d136dfd5bf05edfe2405cc2501828de49319fb313615e7c1e87d4634ec1"} {"question" "q-94275" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075550/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce3cfd52bc88b8e65e4e21d07121bdfe56fe072f3559a7730c852f65ca34f434"} {"question" "q-94275" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075550/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce3cfd52bc88b8e65e4e21d07121bdfe56fe072f3559a7730c852f65ca34f434"} {"question" "q-94275" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075550/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce3cfd52bc88b8e65e4e21d07121bdfe56fe072f3559a7730c852f65ca34f434"} {"question" "q-94286" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=18\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075550/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce3cfd52bc88b8e65e4e21d07121bdfe56fe072f3559a7730c852f65ca34f434"} {"question" "q-94286" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{18}{3}=6.\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6y=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075550/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce3cfd52bc88b8e65e4e21d07121bdfe56fe072f3559a7730c852f65ca34f434"} {"question" "q-94286" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075550/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7cab2d136dfd5bf05edfe2405cc2501828de49319fb313615e7c1e87d4634ec1"} {"question" "q-94286" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075550/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce3cfd52bc88b8e65e4e21d07121bdfe56fe072f3559a7730c852f65ca34f434"} {"question" "q-94286" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}^{″}+6y=0"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+9y=0"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{2}y=0"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{3}y=0"] ["math" "{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{6}y=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075550/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce3cfd52bc88b8e65e4e21d07121bdfe56fe072f3559a7730c852f65ca34f434"} {"question" "q-94286" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{″}+6y=0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075550/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce3cfd52bc88b8e65e4e21d07121bdfe56fe072f3559a7730c852f65ca34f434"} {"question" "q-94287" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle of mass $6\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=24\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. \n\nIf the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, then which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The governing differential equation is ${y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0$\n- The frequency $f$ of the oscillation is $f=\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}$\n- The general solution can be expressed as $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$ where $R$ is the amplitude and $ϕ$ is the phase" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075550/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce3cfd52bc88b8e65e4e21d07121bdfe56fe072f3559a7730c852f65ca34f434"} {"question" "q-94287" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{24}{6}=4\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2.\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=0.\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is false.\n\n- The frequency $f$ (measured in ${s}^{-1}$) is given by\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{1}{T}=\\frac{\\omega }{2\\pi },\n$$\n\nwhere $T$ is the period. So in our case, we have\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{2}{2\\pi }=\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\,.\n$$\n\nSo the frequency $f=\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\nHence, statement II is false.\n\n- The general solution for $y(t)$ can take one of three forms.\n\n- $A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\nWriting $\\omega =2$ in form 1 gives\n\n$$\ny(t)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nHence, statement III is true.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075550/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce3cfd52bc88b8e65e4e21d07121bdfe56fe072f3559a7730c852f65ca34f434"} {"question" "q-94287" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075550/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7cab2d136dfd5bf05edfe2405cc2501828de49319fb313615e7c1e87d4634ec1"} {"question" "q-94287" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075550/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce3cfd52bc88b8e65e4e21d07121bdfe56fe072f3559a7730c852f65ca34f434"} {"question" "q-94287" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075550/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce3cfd52bc88b8e65e4e21d07121bdfe56fe072f3559a7730c852f65ca34f434"} {"question" "q-94287" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075550/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce3cfd52bc88b8e65e4e21d07121bdfe56fe072f3559a7730c852f65ca34f434"}] "usage" {"q-64218" []} "basis" 2756 "retractions" [[17592186148078 :question/problem "The position $y(t)$, measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$y^{″} + 4y = 0$,\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $2m/s$, find the amplitude of the oscillation."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186146480 17592186146481 17592186146482 17592186146483 17592186146484] "immutable_fields" [[17592186146479 :answer-field/correct] [17592186146479 :answer-field/id] [17592186146479 :answer-field/key] [17592186146479 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
